Changing Files and Directory Permission:
When we do ls -ltr we get to know what permission we have for files and directory, the output of above will give like below
-rwxrw-r--
drwxrwxrwx
There are 3 types of permission
r-read
w-write
x-execute(program file)
Above rwx can be give to below
u - user permission
g - group permission
o - others permission
Below two what it indicate is
-rwxrw-r--
- is file first rwx ie first 3 is user which have all read write and execute access.if x present in file then it may be program file, second 3(rw-) which indicate group, which have only read and write access and dose not have execute access. last three(r--) which indicate others, which have only read access and dose not have write and execute access
drwxrwxrwx
d means directory above is same applicable for directory. in directory if x is present, it means it dosent have any program file, it means it allow to cd into this directory. if x dosent have it wont allow you to go inside

chmod - change mod - this command help you to provide permission or remove permisssion to files and directory
+ To add permisssion
- To remove permission

To add write permisssion to file or directory for user
chmod u+w kala(here kala is file)
To add write permisssion to file or directory for group
chmod g+w kala(here kala is file)
To add write and read permisssion to file or directory for group
chmod g+rw kala(here kala is file)
To add write and read and execute permisssion to file or directory for group. same for other and user as well, just change u and o instead of g
chmod g+rwx kala(here kala is file)
To give all user, group and other any permission just use a
chmod a+rwx kala
All combinations are possible like below
chmod ugo+rwx kala

To remove permission just same as add , just use - instead of +
chmod a-rwx kala

Change File or Directory permission using numbers:
0 - No permission
1 - Execute
2 - Write
3 - Execute+Write
4 - Read
5 - Read+Execute
6 - Read+Write
7 - Read+Write+Execute
It is same as above
chmod 777 file first 7 is user, second 7 is group and third 7 is other. So from this example all user, group and other will have all rwx access
chmod 765 kala(file or directory name), user have all rwx, group have rw, and other have rx

File Ownership:
Ownership of a file or directory can be with user and group. if we do ls-ltr it will show which user and which group have ownership of that file and directory
To change the ownership of file and directory, only root user can be able to do
First switch to root user
su -
Change Ownership of file or directory:
chown username filename
chown root abinaya
Change Group of file or directory:
chgrp username filename
chgrp root abinaya

If we are in /home/drajendran folder that is the user folder not a root folder, post we changed the above file user and group to root, if we try to remove the file, it will delete, eventhough it was changed to root. The reason is it was inside the drajendran folder, so the drajendran user will still have access to delete the file

Access Control List:
ACL will be used , when we need to give a specific permission to particular user or group.Eg there is file called abinaya, if we need to give access to that file , it can give on user group level or other level, but not on particular user. if we give access to that file om other or group, all user and all group will get access to that file. inorder to overcome that we have this acl
To give permission or remove we can use setfacl (set file acl)
to list the permission we can use getfacl (get file acl)
setfacl permission
Give rwx permission to user
setfacl -m u:drajendran:rwx /path of file with file name here u is user, drajendran is username , rwx is read write and execute permission
Give rwx permission to group
setfacl -m g:drajendran:rmx /pathof file with file name here g is user, drajendran is username , rwx is read write and execute permission
Give all subfolder file permission
setfacl -Rm u:drajendran:rmx /pathof file with file name here is R is recursive
To remove user
setfacl -x u:drajendran / path of file with file name
to remove all user
setfacl -b /PATH OF FILE NAME
To get the what are permission that file have
getfacl filename

