Cut Command:
Cut command is used to extract a part of line from file or piped data and print output in cli.We can cut the lines in cut command by using delimiter, character, and byte size
To get version of cut command cut --version
To display the all lines first character alone
cut -c1 filename
To display the all lines first 5 character
cut -c1-5 filename
To display the all lines last 5 character
cut -c-5 filename
To display the all lines first 5 character and last 5 character
cut -c1-5 -c-5 filename
To display the all lines last 5 character and first 5 character
cut -c-5 -c1-5 filename
To Display all lines 1,5 ,7 character
cut -c1,5,7 filename
To display all lines based on character range
cut -c1-5, 7-9 filename
To Display all lines based on bytes position
cut -b1 filename
To Display all lines based on bytes position with range
cut -b1-3 filename
To display based on delimiter
cut -d: -f1 filename here f1 is field 1 ie column 1, : is delimiter used here
To display based on delimiter with range
cut -d: -f1-3 filename ie column i to 3
To display based on piped output
ls -ltr | cut -d: -f1

awk Command:
awk is used to extract the data or field from file or piped data
synatax awk 'search criteria {action}' filename
eg:
awk '{print $1}' file this will print first column in file
awk '{print $1, $4}' file  This will print first and 4th column in file
awk '{print $0}' file   This $0 will print everything
awk '/Desk/ ${print $0}' file This will print lines which contains desk
awk -F- '{print $1}' file  This will print first column with delimiter -
awk 'total($0)>15' file This will print lines which total is greater than 15
Based on piped data
ls -ltr | awk '{print $1}' same as above but it will print first column based on piped data.rest all above can be used with piped data
Can use IF condiions
ls -ltr | awk '{if($9='Desktop') print $0}'  this will print lines which contains Desktop on the column 9
cat file | awk '{$2="new"; print $0}' This will replace 2nd column with new
ls -ltr | awk '{print $NF}' This will print last column
ls -ltr | awk '{print NF}' this will print each line has how many columns

grep/egrep Command:
It used to search particular from file or piped data(ie output)
syntax grep keyword file
grep kala filename search kala from file name and print back output of that line
grep -c kala filename search kala from file name and print back count of the occurrence
grep -i kala filename which ignore case sensitive and print result with both kala and Kala
grep -n kala filename search for kala and print the line with line number
grep -v kala filename print except kala result
grep -vi kala filename ignore case sensitive and print except kala results
grep -v kala filename | awk '{print $1}' | cut -c1-3 here apart from kala take everything then this as input and passed to awk '{print $1}', this awk will take first column and pass as input to cut command and that cut command will take first three character and print the output
ls -ltr | grep -i desktop search ignored case sensitive desktop from output of ls -ltr and print result
egrep:
It used to search two or more keywords at a time
egrep -i "keyword1|keyword2| keyword3" filename
all above can be done using egrep too

sort/uniq command:
sort  - used to sort in alphabetical order both file and piped data(ie output)
uniq - used to remove duplicates
syntax - sort filename which sort in alphabetical order
sort -r filename sort in reverse alphabetical order
sort -k2 filename it sort based on second field or column in alphabetical order
ls -ltr | sort | awk '{print $NF}' this will do ls -ltr then that op pass as input to sort, it will sort based on last column with alphabetical order. since awk '{print $NF}' will consider the last column. which is same as sort -K9 filename
uniq - cannot use unique alone .if we need to use uniq need to use sort before
sort filename | uniq it will show only uniq value
sort filename | uniq -c it will show all the uniq value and its count
sort filename | uniq -d it will show what are the duplicate value present in the file

wc command:
It used to count the number of words, lines and byte count in file and piped data ie command output
syntax:
wc filename it will will give all line, word and bytes count
wc -l filename will give only line count
wc -w filename give only word count
wc -c filename give only bytes count
It can be used with piped data as well
ls -ltr | wc -l by doing this it will give number of files and directory in specified folder. always minus one with total count hence ls -ltr output give first line with total count which should be ignored which is not file and directory
ls -ltr | grep drw| wc -l this will give only directory count. we are greping only drw which is d is directory with rand w permission then getting that count alone using wc -l

